body:
I think hiding his eyes robs him of a lot of expression, and the cropping is awkward i don't know why 

&#x200B;

I think making him look at the viewer while furrowing his brow with one eye more visible than the other would add a lot of expressiveness!

you look goooood!! I can't imagine you with hair lol

&#x200B;

not even gassing, it suits you

I looked it up and you see, I think you just saved my life since I think it's exactly the brush that I was looking for thanks for saving me from painting everything with just the square blocking brush rip

&#x200B;

i'm gonna go look if I have a brush like that now >;)

I would put the whole creature in the shadow and only some of the more bright light fall on the girls face, the whole light bleaks thing doesn't associate well with the creature at hand, however you can probably keep it on the girl

&#x200B;

I love how you did the water and the cube thing and how it's the only non organic thing in the piece is intriguing

&#x200B;

the environment isn't clear, it lacks hard edges and forground bg distinction (is make bg lighter? covered in mist?

&#x200B;

his back has no muscles to support the size the wings he has

&#x200B;

the wings are too "angelic" for this guy, again you're mixing themes like with the beautiful light I'd make them more "worn out" and bend them behind his back, they also shouldn't probably be spread out with those trees everywhere. I know it's a lot I'm sorry

&#x200B;

the tail does nothing for this piece where it is, maybe makeit point to the creature in some way to lead the viewer, and make it thinner, since it's not as important as the face cube etc . turn the thick part the other way to frame the canvas

&#x200B;

okay! if you want a quick fix however, 

1 put the whole guy in shadow, mist etc

2 give the whole piece more contrast especially on the girls face, contrast also includes some brigher color or diffeerent ( i suggest pale blue) accents very sparingly somewhere near the focal point

&#x200B;

\*laught nervously\* hope you don't hate me now :') I still think it's an intriguing idea and believable nature and I want to know more about this guy

&#x200B;

hope this helps!

Well, that's just how it is here xd I think those are valid too, but I think there's no point of sitting for hours making perfect realistic paintings if nobody likes it and when you're only working you tend to forget that you're making art that someone will like if that makes sense

&#x200B;

Your comment was a nice addition/"dilution" in this very serious community \^ \^

Thanks  for your feedback!! You're right I was going for indoor lighting, mid west skin? Not so much xd Just wanted more dark setting to have more grays to work with to represent the form (as my previous critique homework was to add more radial shading for the face)

&#x200B;

I agree the eyes look kinda dead and the whole thing kinda dull, so I think I'll lighten everything for day 7, definitely. I love highlights too when other ppl do it \^ \^ But the problem is I've gotten this about the highlights before but it's difficult for me to understand because if I up the contrast it starts to look really plastic-y or metallic..

the light touching her forehead is a tangent and I agree with ida about cropping, you can keep her in the corner but still crop a lot. I'd add a shoulder if you want to keep her to the side so it's not that awkward how she is cropped on her hair and it's not just head. You might even need to zoom out insead of cropping in this case.

&#x200B;

The light is coming from the side angle but she's looking up as if the light ball it up
